## Proof

We use **Kronecker substitution**: encode the bivariate product as a univariate multiplication in $R[X]$ by substituting a suitable power of $X$ for $Y$, then decode the result.

### Setup

Write $g = \sum_{i=0}^{m-1} g_i(X)\,Y^i$ and $h = \sum_{i=0}^{m-1} h_i(X)\,Y^i$ with $\deg_X g_i, \deg_X h_i < k$. The product is

$$f = gh = \sum_{i=0}^{2m-2} f_i(X)\,Y^i, \qquad f_i = \sum_{j=\max(0,i-m+1)}^{\min(i,m-1)} g_j\,h_{i-j}.$$

Since each $g_j, h_{i-j}$ has $X$-degree $< k$, every product $g_j h_{i-j}$ has $X$-degree $< 2k-1$, hence

$$\deg_X f_i < 2k-1 \qquad\text{for all } i. \tag{$\star$}$$

### The substitution

Define the ring homomorphism $\phi : R[X,Y] \to R[X]$ by $\phi(X)=X$ and $\phi(Y)=X^{2k}$. Concretely,

$$\phi(g) = \sum_{i=0}^{m-1} g_i(X)\,X^{2ki}, \qquad \phi(h) = \sum_{i=0}^{m-1} h_i(X)\,X^{2ki}.$$

Because $\deg_X g_i < k$, the $i$-th summand $g_i(X)X^{2ki}$ occupies exactly the coefficient range $[2ki,\; 2ki+k-1]$, and consecutive ranges are separated by a gap of $k$ positions. Hence $\phi$ is **injective** on the set of polynomials $\sum a_i Y^i$ with $\deg_X a_i < k$ (the $g_i$ can be read off disjointly). Likewise for $h$.

**Degrees.** The top term of $\phi(g)$ is $g_{m-1}(X)\,X^{2k(m-1)}$ with $\deg_X g_{m-1}<k$, so

$$\deg_X \phi(g) < 2k(m-1)+k = 2km - k < 2km.$$

Similarly $\deg_X \phi(h) < 2km$.

### Multiplication

Since $\phi$ is a homomorphism, $\phi(f) = \phi(g)\,\phi(h)$. Both factors have $X$-degree $< 2km$, so this single univariate multiplication costs $M(2km)$ operations in $R$.

### Decoding

Using $(\star)$,

$$\phi(f) = \sum_{i=0}^{2m-2} f_i(X)\,X^{2ki}, \qquad \deg_X f_i < 2k-1.$$

The $i$-th summand occupies the coefficient range $[2ki,\; 2ki + 2k - 2]$, which has width $2k-1$. The next summand begins at position $2k(i+1) = 2ki + 2k$. Since

$$2ki + 2k - 2 \;<\; 2ki + 2k,$$

consecutive ranges **do not overlap**. Therefore each $f_i$ is recovered uniquely and directly by reading off the $2k$ consecutive coefficients of $\phi(f)$ starting at index $2ki$ (the last position in each block is always zero). This extraction costs $O(km)$ operations (there are $2m-1$ blocks of size $O(k)$).

### Cost analysis

- Constructing $\phi(g), \phi(h)$: $O(km)$ (relabeling/placing coefficients).
- Multiplying: $M(2km)$.
- Extracting $f_0,\dots,f_{2m-2}$: $O(km)$.

By the standard superlinearity assumption on $M$ (satisfied by schoolbook, Karatsuba, FFT-based, and all usual multiplication algorithms), $M(2n) = O(M(n))$; in particular $M(2km)=O(M(km))$. Also $M(km)\geq km$ since the input must at least be read. Hence

$$\text{Total} = M(2km) + O(km) = O(M(km)) + O(M(km)) = O(M(km)).$$

### Conclusion

Given $g$ and $h$, form $\phi(g),\phi(h)\in R[X]$ by substituting $Y\mapsto X^{2k}$, multiply them as univariate polynomials of degree $<2km$ in $R[X]$, and read off each $f_i$ from the disjoint coefficient blocks of the product. The entire computation uses $O(M(km))$ operations in $R$.

